```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <errno.h>
#include <string.h>

int myFunction() {
    // Some operation that might fail
    errno = ERANGE; // Set an error code
    return 0; // Return 0 to indicate an error occurred
}

int main() {
    if (myFunction() == 0) {
        printf("An error occurred: %s\n", strerror(errno));
    }
    return 0;
}
```

In this case, `myFunction` sets `errno` to `ERANGE` and returns `0` to indicate an error. The calling code then uses `strerror` to retrieve a human-readable error message.

Both methods are valid, and the choice between them depends on your specific needs and the context of your application.

